/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://vwacjfsalvbyokqvhzes.supabase.co wss://vwacjfsalvbyokqvhzes.supabase.co

# Vite content-hashes these filenames (index-kZ4pQXV3.js), so a changed file
# is always a *different* URL. That makes them safe to cache forever, and
# Netlify's default of `max-age=0, must-revalidate` was making the browser
# re-check ~450 KB of JS on every single launch.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Notes on the CSP above, since a wrong policy breaks the app silently:
#
# connect-src  Supabase is the only external origin the bundle actually
#              talks to. BOTH schemes are required and they are not
#              interchangeable: https:// for REST + auth, wss:// for the
#              Realtime websocket that drives live message sync. Dropping
#              the wss:// entry breaks live updates only *after* sign-in —
#              exactly the kind of thing a quick smoke test misses.
#              (github.com and reactjs.org also appear in the bundle, but
#              only inside React's error-message strings — never fetched.)
#
# style-src    'unsafe-inline' is unavoidable: React writes inline `style`
#              attributes throughout. The tighter split of
#              `style-src 'self'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'` was
#              considered and rejected — a browser that doesn't recognise
#              style-src-attr falls back to style-src, which would strip
#              every inline style and leave the app looking wrecked on
#              older iOS. Not worth that risk: there is no innerHTML
#              anywhere in the app (src/markdown.tsx builds React nodes,
#              and its link regex hard-requires https?://, so javascript:
#              URLs can't get through), so the XSS surface this would be
#              defending is nil.
#
# script-src   No 'unsafe-eval' needed — verified zero eval()/new Function()
#              in the production bundle, and Vite emits no inline <script>.
#
# worker-src   Covers navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js'), which is
#              what makes add-to-home-screen and push work at all.
